  "description": "A parametric UPHOLSTERED PANEL on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT. This is the plan's hero wall: a floor-to-cove channelled panel that floats off the wall with an LED halo behind it. It is deliberately NOT part of the bed — `bed-with-math.headboardHeight` is a board bolted to a bed, and a 2.74 × 2.97 m wall feature is a different thing that must survive the bed being moved.\n\nALL FOUR PATTERNS ARE ONE BROADCAST. The pad field is a grid of `_nu` columns by `_nz` rows, and the pattern only decides whether each count is a division or a 1 — vertical is `_nz = 1`, horizontal is `_nu = 1`, square grid is both, plain is both with the groove set to zero. There is no second body for any of them, which is why changing pattern cannot change anything else about the panel.\n\nTHE GROOVE IS NOT DRAWN. A groove is the gap the pads leave, subtracted half from each side of every pad so the field still fills the frame exactly whatever the count. Drawing the groove as a solid would double-count the same millimetres.\n\nTHE FLOAT GAP IS WHAT MAKES THE HALO POSSIBLE. `_fg` pushes the backer off the wall face, and the halo frame lives in that gap and nowhere else — set the gap to zero and the halo drops out on its own rather than being clipped into the wall.\n\nTHE LIP SHRINKS THE PAD FIELD, it does not overlay it: `_fu0 = _u0 + _lw`. Turn the lip off and the pads take the full face, so the two can never overlap.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: 2.743 × 2.972 m at 0.133 m total depth, 0.064 m off the wall, 27 vertical channels at 0.101 m pitch, 8.15 m² of face and 11.2 m of halo.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. Pads are flat-fronted quads — there is no crowned or buttoned cushion, and a diamond or chevron pattern is not a grid so it is not here. The lip is square, on two sides and the top only.",
